From 4d634d500b073282e3da6bdb37c099f6a7270cc8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 09:25:05 +0000
Subject: net: ethernet: lantiq: CBM interrupt handlers and host-RX engine

Add the CBM interrupt layer and the tasklet-based host receive
engine (cbm_intr.c):

 - The five CBM interrupt lines (cbm_isr_0/4/5/6/7) with the
   read/dispatch/write-1-to-clear acknowledge discipline for the
   FSQM, EQM-CPU-port and DQM-CPU-port sub-sources. Lines 4-7
   additionally carry the per-CPU load-spreader (LS) sources: on
   an LS interrupt the line's LS sources are masked and the
   matching RX tasklet scheduled. All four LS ports (0-3) are
   serviced - the load spreader distributes host-RX across them,
   and leaving any port unserviced (only port 0 was originally
   wired) turns its first frame into a silent hard-IRQ storm that
   wedges the box with no console output.

 - The RX engine (do_cbm_tasklet): drain the LS output ring with a
   budget, validate each descriptor before trusting it (a
   data_len of zero or beyond segment size drops the frame instead
   of crashing the skb path), invalidate the CPU cache for the
   buffer through its memremap-provided VA (the pool is mapped
   memory, not phys - invalidating by physical address is a no-op
   and delivers stale cache lines as corrupted frames), copy-break
   into a fresh skb, strip the 8-byte PMAC header, deliver via
   netif_receive_skb, and recycle the segment to the FSQM pool.
   Rescheduling is bounded so a stuck ring cannot livelock the
   CPU in softirq.

 - cbm_rx_engine_init() arms the four tasklets at probe;
   cbm_rx_set_netdev() binds/unbinds the delivering netdev at
   ndo_open/ndo_stop (frames arriving with no netdev bound are
   dropped and recycled).

Derived from the AVM FRITZ!Box 7560 GPL release (Linux 4.9.198),
cqm/grx500/cbm.c interrupt/LS code (no vendor copyright banners in
the origin); the descriptor validation, cache-maintenance fix and
bounded reschedule come from this port's hardware bring-up.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
